          "number": "1",
          "title": "Big Picture & Concept Explanation (Simple Language)",
          "markdown": "![Newton's Rings and Michelson Interferometer](/notes/diagrams/u1-newtons-rings-michelson.svg)\n\n\n## 1. Big Picture & Concept Explanation (Simple Language)\n\nLight is not just a straight ray—it is a transverse electromagnetic **wave** made of oscillating electric and magnetic fields. When light passes through narrow gaps, hits thin transparent layers, or encounters crystals, it shows three core wave behaviors that simple ray optics cannot explain:\n\n1. **Interference (Two waves adding up):** When two coherent light waves overlap, their crests add up to give **bright** regions and crest-meets-trough cancels out to give **dark** regions. By splitting a single wave's **amplitude** (half reflected, half transmitted at a boundary—called *division of amplitude*), thin soap films, **Newton's rings**, and the **Michelson interferometer** turn tiny distances (fractions of a micron!) into easily countable light and dark fringes.\n2. **Diffraction (Waves bending around corners):** When a plane wave passes through a narrow slit or a **diffraction grating** (thousands of parallel slits), wavelets from different parts of the same wavefront bend and interfere. That spreads light into a broad central bright band flanked by weak side peaks. Because every wavelength bends by a slightly different angle, gratings split light into ultra-sharp color spectra.\n3. **Rayleigh Criterion & Resolving Power:** Diffraction blurs every star or spectral line into a tiny fuzzy spot (Airy disk). **Rayleigh's criterion** tells us the minimum separation needed so two close stars in a **telescope** or two close wavelengths in a **grating** still look like two separate peaks instead of merging into one blur.\n4. **Polarization (Vibration direction):** Light normally vibrates in all planes perpendicular to its travel direction (unpolarized). Passing it through special **double-refracting crystals** like calcite (or a **Nicol prism**) selects just one vibration plane—giving **plane-polarized light**."
