          "id": "ch-5-exam-questions-with-complete-answers",
          "number": "5",
          "title": "Exam Questions (With Complete Answers)",
          "markdown": "## 5. Exam Questions (With Complete Answers)\n\n### Short Answer Questions (2 Marks Each)\n\n1. **Why do two independent bulbs never produce sustained interference?**\n   **Answer:** Independent light sources emit photons from random atomic transitions whose phase difference changes roughly every $10^{-8}\\text{ s}$ (*incoherent*). Thus bright and dark regions swap billions of times per second and average out to uniform illumination.\n2. **Why is a thick film unable to show interference colors in white light?**\n   **Answer:** For large thickness $t$, many different wavelengths simultaneously satisfy the constructive condition $2\\mu t\\cos r = (2n+1)\\lambda/2$ across the visible spectrum. All colors reflect equally and overlap back into **white light**.\n3. **Why is the center of Newton's rings dark in reflected light?**\n   **Answer:** At the point of contact, air film thickness $t = 0$. Reflection from the denser flat glass plate below introduces a Stokes' phase shift of $\\pi$ (path difference $\\lambda/2$), which meets the condition for destructive interference.\n4. **What happens in single-slit diffraction if the slit width $a < \\lambda$?**\n   **Answer:** The first minimum condition $\\sin\\theta = \\lambda/a > 1$ has no real solution. Hence **no diffraction minima form**; the central maximum spreads uniformly across the whole $180^\\circ$ forward half-space.\n5. **Distinguish between O-ray and E-ray inside a doubly refracting crystal.**\n   **Answer:** The **O-ray** obeys Snell's law, travels with equal speed in all directions (spherical wavefront), and has constant $\\mu_o$. The **E-ray** generally violates Snell's law, travels with direction-dependent speed (ellipsoidal wavefront), and its refractive index $\\mu_e$ varies with direction.\n\n### Long Answer Questions (7–10 Marks Each)\n\n1. **Explain Newton's rings experiment with a labeled diagram. Derive $D_n^2 = 4n\\lambda R$ for dark rings and explain how it measures the refractive index of a liquid.** *(Use the Newton's Rings & Michelson Interferometer diagram above and Derivation §2.2 above, ending with $\\mu = (\\Delta D^2)_{\\text{air}} / (\\Delta D^2)_{\\text{liq}}$.)*\n2. **Discuss Fraunhofer diffraction at a single slit. Derive $I = I_0 (\\frac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\alpha})^2$ and show that the intensity of the first secondary maximum is about $4.5\\%$ ($1/22$) of the central peak.** *(Use the Diffraction & Polarization diagram above and Derivation §2.4: at $\\alpha \\approx 3\\pi/2$, $I_1/I_0 = (\\frac{1}{3\\pi/2})^2 = \\frac{4}{9\\pi^2} \\approx 0.0450 = 4.5\\% \\approx 1/22$.)*\n3. **Explain the construction and working of a Nicol prism with a neat diagram. How does it produce and analyze plane-polarized light?** *(Include crystal angle $68^\\circ$, Canada Balsam $\\mu = 1.55$, $\\mu_o = 1.658 > \\mu_B > \\mu_e = 1.486$, TIR of O-ray, transmission of E-ray, and using a second Nicol prism as an analyzer where complete darkness occurs when optic axes are crossed.)*"
