          "markdown": "![Ruby & He-Ne Lasers](/notes/diagrams/u2-lasers-ruby-hene.svg)\n\n\n## 1. Big Picture & Concept Explanation (Simple Language)\n\nNormal light bulbs spray photons randomly in all directions and wavelengths because atoms emit independently (*spontaneous emission*). A **LASER** (*Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation*) forces millions of excited atoms to release **identical, in-step photons** together inside a mirror cavity (*optical resonator*). That creates a beam with four superpowers: extreme **monochromaticity** (one pure color), high **coherence** (waves locked in phase), high **directionality** (tiny divergence), and high **intensity**.\n\nOnce we generate laser light, how do we send gigabits of data across oceans without electrical cables? Through hair-thin silica glass threads called **optical fibres**. By coating a high-index glass **core** ($n_1$) with a slightly lower-index **cladding** ($n_2 < n_1$), any light entering within the **acceptance cone** bounces cleanly down the fibre via **Total Internal Reflection (TIR)**—completely immune to electromagnetic interference (EMI) and lightning!"
