          "number": "5",
          "title": "Exam Questions (With Complete Answers)",
          "markdown": "## 5. Exam Questions (With Complete Answers)\n\n### Short Answer Questions (2 Marks Each)\n\n1. **Why did Maxwell modify Ampère's circuital law?**\n   **Answer:** Taking divergence of $\\nabla\\times\\vec{H} = \\vec{J}_c$ forces $\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{J}_c = 0$, contradicting the continuity equation $\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{J}_c = -\\partial\\rho/\\partial t$ in time-varying circuits like charging capacitors. Adding displacement current $\\vec{J}_d = \\partial\\vec{D}/\\partial t$ fixes the contradiction.\n2. **State the physical meaning of $\\nabla\\cdot\\vec{B} = 0$.**\n   **Answer:** Isolated magnetic monopoles do not exist; every magnetic field line forms a continuous closed loop with net outward magnetic flux through any closed surface equal to zero.\n3. **Why does inserting a dielectric increase a capacitor's capacitance?**\n   **Answer:** Induced dipoles create an internal bound electric field opposing the applied field, reducing net $E$ and plate voltage $V$ for the same free charge $Q$, so $C = Q/V$ increases by $\\epsilon_r$.\n4. **Define wave impedance of free space and state its value.**\n   **Answer:** Wave impedance is the ratio of electric to magnetic field strength in a plane wave: $\\eta_0 = E/H = \\sqrt{\\mu_0/\\epsilon_0} = 120\\pi \\approx 376.73\\ \\Omega$.\n5. **Distinguish between soft and hard ferromagnetic materials.**\n   **Answer:** *Soft* ferromagnets (silicon steel) have narrow $B\\text{–}H$ hysteresis loops, low coercivity, and low core loss (used in transformers). *Hard* ferromagnets (Alnico, NdFeB) have wide loops and high retentivity/coercivity (used for permanent magnets).\n\n### Long Answer Questions (7–10 Marks Each)\n\n1. **Write all four Maxwell equations in differential and integral forms, explain their physical significance, and explain how displacement current resolves the charging capacitor paradox.** *(Include §2.3 equations + the Electromagnetism diagram above, part (a).)*\n2. **Derive the EM wave equations in free space ($\\nabla^2\\vec{E} = \\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\partial^2\\vec{E}/\\partial t^2$) from Maxwell's equations. Show that EM waves travel at speed $c$ and are transverse in nature.** *(Include §2.4 + the Electromagnetism diagram above, part (b).)*\n3. **State and prove Poynting's Theorem and explain the physical meaning of each term. Compare diamagnetic, paramagnetic, and ferromagnetic materials.** *(Combine §2.5 proof + Table §2.2.)*"
